42	11	Quality metrics definition	ROLE:\nYou are a senior Test Architect specializing in defining and implementing\nquality metrics frameworks for Native iOS, Android, and web application programmes.\n\nCONTEXT:\nProject: [Project Name]\nPlatform: iOS | Android | Web | All\nProgramme Stage: Project kickoff | Mid-programme | Pre-release | Ongoing BAU\nAudience: Engineering Team | QA Team | Product | Executive / Stakeholders | All\nReporting Tool: JIRA | TestRail | Custom Dashboard | Confluence | [Other]\nCurrent Metrics: None defined | Ad hoc — [describe] | Formal — [describe]\nImprovement Goal: [e.g., Reduce escaped defects | Improve release confidence |\n                  Increase automation ROI | Reduce time to detect]\n\nTASK:\nUsing the programme context and improvement goals provided below, define a\ncomplete quality metrics framework covering all aspects of the testing programme.\n\nYou MUST cover ALL of the following — do not skip any:\n  1. Metrics selection — the right metrics for the stated goals, with justification\n  2. Metric definitions — precise, unambiguous definitions with calculation formulas\n  3. Baseline and targets — current baseline (if known) and improvement targets\n  4. Data collection — where each metric comes from and how it is collected\n  5. Reporting cadence — how often each metric is reported and to whom\n  6. Dashboard design — what each audience sees and in what format\n  7. Metrics anti-patterns — common vanity metrics to avoid and why\n  8. Metrics evolution — how the framework adapts as the programme matures\n\nEvaluate the FULL programme context and improvement goals before selecting any metric.\nEvery metric must directly support at least one improvement goal — remove any that do not.\nDo NOT include vanity metrics that look good but do not drive behaviour change.\nIf baseline data is unavailable, flag as [NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] — do not fabricate numbers.\n\nPROGRAMME CONTEXT AND IMPROVEMENT GOALS:\n[Paste programme overview, current quality pain points, and specific improvement goals here]\n\nCONSTRAINTS:\n- Every metric must have a precise mathematical definition — not a narrative description\n- Targets must be specific and time-bound — not "improve over time"\n- Data collection method must be automated where possible — manual collection is a risk\n- Dashboard must be tailored per audience — executives do not need the same view as engineers\n- Anti-patterns section must call out any metric in this framework that could become a vanity metric if misused\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nQUALITY METRICS FRAMEWORK\nProgramme: [Name] | Platform: [Platform] | Version: [X.X]\nPrepared By: [Test Architect Name] | Date: [DD/MM/YYYY] | Review Cycle: [e.g., Quarterly]\n\n---\n\n1. METRICS SELECTION:\n| Metric | Category | Improvement Goal Supported | Justification for Inclusion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Metric name] | [e.g., Coverage / Defect / Efficiency] | [Goal it supports] | [Why this metric matters here] |\n\n---\n\n2. METRIC DEFINITIONS:\nMETRIC: [Metric Name]\nCategory: Coverage | Defect | Efficiency | Automation | Process\nDefinition: [Plain English definition — one sentence]\nFormula: [Metric Name] = [Numerator] / [Denominator] x 100\nExample: [Worked example with real numbers]\nMeasured At: [e.g., End of each sprint | Per release | Daily]\nData Source: [e.g., JIRA defect count | TestRail execution results | CI pipeline]\nCollection Method: Automated | Manual | Semi-automated\nOwner: [Role responsible for this metric]\n\n---\n(repeat for every metric)\n\n---\n\n3. BASELINE AND TARGETS:\n| Metric | Current Baseline | Target | Target Date | Measurement Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Test Coverage | [N% — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | 100% of AC | [Date] | Per sprint |\n| Automation Coverage | [N% — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | [N]% | [Date] | Monthly |\n| Defect Escape Rate | [N — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | 0 Critical | Per release | Per release |\n| Pass Rate | [N% — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | ≥95% | [Date] | Per sprint |\n| Mean Time to Detect | [N days — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | <[N] hours | [Date] | Monthly |\n| Flaky Test Rate | [N% — or NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE] | <2% | [Date] | Weekly |\n| [Additional metric] | | | | |\n\n---\n\n4. DATA COLLECTION:\n| Metric | Data Source | Collection Method | Frequency | Automated? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Test Coverage | TestRail / JIRA | Export via API | Per sprint | Yes |\n| Automation Coverage | CI pipeline report | Script parses results | Per run | Yes |\n| Defect Escape Rate | JIRA — production bugs label | JIRA filter + API | Per release | Yes |\n| Pass Rate | CI pipeline / TestRail | Automated report | Per run | Yes |\n| Mean Time to Detect | JIRA created date vs commit timestamp | Script calculation | Monthly | Semi |\n| Flaky Test Rate | CI pipeline flaky test flag | Automated tagging | Per run | Yes |\n\n---\n\n5. REPORTING CADENCE:\n| Metric | Engineering Team | QA Lead | Product | Executive |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Test Coverage | Per sprint | Per sprint | Per release | Per quarter |\n| Automation Coverage | Monthly | Monthly | Per quarter | Per quarter |\n| Defect Escape Rate | Per release | Per release | Per release | Per release |\n| Pass Rate | Per run | Per sprint | Per release | Per release |\n| Mean Time to Detect | Monthly | Monthly | Per quarter | Per quarter |\n| Flaky Test Rate | Weekly | Weekly | — | — |\n\n---\n\n6. DASHBOARD DESIGN:\nENGINEERING TEAM DASHBOARD:\nPurpose: Day-to-day visibility of test health and pipeline status\nMetrics Shown: Pass rate, flaky test rate, automation coverage, open defects by severity\nFormat: Live CI dashboard | JIRA board | [Tool]\nRefresh Rate: Real-time | Per run\n\nQA LEAD DASHBOARD:\nPurpose: Sprint and release quality tracking\nMetrics Shown: All engineering metrics + test coverage, defect escape rate, mean time to detect\nFormat: TestRail report | JIRA dashboard | [Tool]\nRefresh Rate: Daily\n\nPRODUCT DASHBOARD:\nPurpose: Release readiness and quality trend visibility\nMetrics Shown: Pass rate trend, defect escape rate, release sign-off status\nFormat: Confluence page | Custom report | [Tool]\nRefresh Rate: Per sprint / Per release\n\nEXECUTIVE DASHBOARD:\nPurpose: Programme-level quality health and trend\nMetrics Shown: Defect escape rate trend, automation ROI, release quality score\nFormat: Single-page summary | Slide deck | [Tool]\nRefresh Rate: Monthly / Per quarter\n\n---\n\n7. METRICS ANTI-PATTERNS:\n| Anti-Pattern | Why It Is Harmful | Safe Alternative |\n|---|---|---|\n| Test case count as a quality measure | More tests ≠ better quality — encourages padding | Use test coverage against AC instead |\n| 100% pass rate as success | May indicate insufficient test challenge, not quality | Track pass rate alongside defect escape rate |\n| Defect count as team performance KPI | Discourages defect reporting — hides quality problems | Use defect escape rate and time to detect instead |\n| Automation percentage without ROI | High automation % means nothing if tests are flaky or irrelevant | Pair automation coverage with flaky test rate and maintenance cost |\n| [Additional anti-pattern] | [Why harmful] | [Safe alternative] |\n\n---\n\n8. METRICS EVOLUTION:\n| Programme Stage | Active Metrics | Retire | Add |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Project kickoff | Coverage, pass rate, defect count | — | — |\n| Mid-programme | All above + automation coverage, flaky rate | Defect count (replace with escape rate) | Mean time to detect |\n| Pre-release | All above + escape rate, release readiness score | — | Release quality score |\n| BAU / Scaled | Stable set + automation ROI | Sprint-level coverage (replace with trend) | Cost of quality |\n\n---\n\nBASELINES NEEDING CAPTURE:\n| Metric | Action | Owner | By Date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| [Any NEEDS BASELINE CAPTURE items] | Instrument data collection | [Role] | [DD/MM/YYYY] |
